  "prompt_spec": {
    "original_prompt": "From Aristotle's works, it is clear that phantasia is causally downstream from aisthesis ontologically: phantasia ontologically depends on aisthesis but is different from it. Articulate the ontological chain of causal dependency, as aristotle understands it, of the following (each \"-->\" illustrates the following item being causally downstream): motion-->time-->aisthesis-->phantasia-->resonant aisthema and pathos -->phantasmata-->thinking, recollection, imagination, etc.",
    "research_questions": [
      "What is the ontological chain of causal dependency in Aristotle's philosophy from motion through time, aisthesis, phantasia, resonant aisthema and pathos, phantasmata, to higher cognitive functions like thinking, recollection, and imagination?"
    ],
    "required_facets": [
      {
        "facet_id": "f83099b0-9cf1-4fc4-9070-a2c79eea4968",
        "name": "Motion and Time as Foundational Ontological Conditions",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish motion (kinesis) as the ontological foundation for time (chronos) in Physics Book IV, and how does this temporal framework provide the necessary conditions for perceptual experience? Examine Aristotle's arguments that time is the measure or number of motion with respect to before and after, and how this metaphysical grounding in Physics creates the ontological substrate required for aisthesis to occur. Investigate passages where Aristotle discusses the relationship between change, continuity, and the temporal dimension of sensory experience.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "711b60ce-a979-4244-b039-f1543221f714",
        "name": "Aisthesis as Causally Dependent on Temporal Motion",
        "description": "What is the causal relationship between time and sense-perception (aisthesis) in Aristotle's De Anima and other psychological works? Examine how Aristotle argues that aisthesis requires temporal duration and motion as ontological preconditions, including his discussions of how sense-perception involves the reception of sensible forms without matter, and how this receptive process necessarily occurs within time. Investigate passages in De Anima Books II and III where Aristotle discusses the actualization of sensory potentiality and the role of temporal process in perceptual discrimination.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "f3425c74-3dc1-4bbc-acb4-5cbc93ccf31c",
        "name": "Phantasia's Ontological Dependence on Aisthesis",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish phantasia (imagination or appearance) as ontologically dependent on but distinct from aisthesis in De Anima Book III chapters 3 and 11, De Memoria, and De Insomniis? Examine Aristotle's explicit statements that phantasia cannot occur without prior sense-perception, his arguments about how phantasia arises from the movement set up by actual sensation, and his careful distinctions between phantasia and aisthesis in terms of truth conditions, voluntary control, and presence in non-rational animals. Investigate the causal mechanism by which completed acts of perception generate the capacity for phantasia.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "e86dfc38-b700-4536-a731-c5a166c822f4",
        "name": "Resonant Aisthema and Pathos as Intermediary Affective State",
        "description": "What is the role of resonant aisthema (retained sense-impressions) and pathos (affective states or passions) in Aristotle's causal chain between phantasia and phantasmata? Examine passages in De Memoria, De Anima, Rhetoric, and Nicomachean Ethics where Aristotle discusses how phantasia produces or is accompanied by pathē (emotions, affections), how sense-impressions persist and resonate after the sensory stimulus is removed, and how these affective and retained perceptual states serve as the material basis for the formation of phantasmata. Investigate Aristotle's account of how emotional and perceptual residues bridge immediate phantasia and stored mental images.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "a186f390-6ec2-430a-a99b-db5fe182f2e3",
        "name": "Phantasmata as Stored Images Enabling Higher Cognition",
        "description": "How does Aristotle characterize phantasmata (mental images or appearances) as the product of resonant aisthema and pathos, and as the necessary foundation for thinking (noēsis), recollection (anamnēsis), and imagination in De Anima Book III chapter 7-8, De Memoria et Reminiscentia, and Metaphysics? Examine Aristotle's famous claim that the soul never thinks without phantasmata, his account of how phantasmata function as internal representations that enable conceptual thought, and his explanation of how memory and recollection operate through chains of phantasmata. Investigate the ontological status of phantasmata as dependent on prior perceptual and affective processes.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      }
    ],
